{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":9083550,"reputation":105,"user_id":39038,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://graph.facebook.com/10157295115100587/picture?type=large","display_name":"Robbie Goodwin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39038/robbie-goodwin"},"edited":false,"score":6,"creation_date":1615151056,"post_id":41358,"comment_id":87063,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It&#39;s never too soon to start reading to a child, but teaching reading is a different thing.  Why not start around the second or third time your daughter seems to show an real interest in the link between spoken words and squiggles on paper?"},{"owner":{"account_id":1507667,"reputation":116,"user_id":28892,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/vGygm.gif?s=256","display_name":"Caius Jard","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/28892/caius-jard"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1615222345,"post_id":41358,"comment_id":87067,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Pretty much the same way you would have someone teach <i>you</i> something; when you show an interest in it. I wonder if you&#39;d have any interest in learning how the CV joints in your car work to allow the engine to transmit power to the wheels in a smooth and uniform fashion while you&#39;re driving in a circle, and why a standard universal joint wouldn&#39;t? Me neither.. :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":20316533,"reputation":853,"user_id":40190,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Gj5plYtcDIQpzM4Ol69AqnbTAojkE1SQnzn7PbH9A=k-s256","display_name":"Isabella Leonarda","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40190/isabella-leonarda"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1615226442,"post_id":41358,"comment_id":87068,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"not really same. Lets face it, do kids express an interest in learning how to use a toilet? Yet they have to be taught or imagine the alternative. So waiting for an interest for some things can result in a lag of development"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1615568341,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":87092,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I voted to close this as likely to elicit opinion rather than evidence. While most permanent memories start forming in early childhood, that doesn&#39;t mean significant stressors (for whatever reason) in babies has no significant effect on later life. The reason probably lies in epigenetic modification of DNA. Several experiments in mice show that a <a href=\"https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/fearful-memories-passed-down/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">fear of the scent of cherry blossoms</a> can be passed on to offspring (!), so it is reasonable that fear in infancy can have ramifications later in life."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1615568447,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":87093,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"This might be a good question for Psychology &amp; Neuroscience.SE."},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user36162"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1615749699,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":87096,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Even if they do not form memories, that&#39;s all the more reason to attend to their wellbeing in the moment. If memories don&#39;t exist, there&#39;s just the present. If the present is distressing, then there&#39;s only distress. Infants, like dementia patients who also don&#39;t make memories, can still have good or bad experiences, good or bad lives. If you&#39;re the other parent, I&#39;d dial back the &quot;I can&#39;t find evidence online that the baby will remember this anyway&quot;. I&#39;d assume the divorce is rooted not the initial crying baby but in the ensuing conflict."},{"owner":{"account_id":4594640,"reputation":121,"user_id":40782,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b4e30985026dd7454af031ec615ecd02?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Allure","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40782/allure"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1615758807,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":87097,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@dxh It wouldn&#39;t be neglecting the baby&#39;s well-being, would it? I suppose it depends on one&#39;s definition of &quot;well-being&quot;, but one could argue that there&#39;s no reason the parent&#39;s presence should actually affect the baby&#39;s well-being. (Also, I am not this baby&#39;s parent and I am not involved in the parenting.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":1946899,"reputation":3943,"user_id":14484,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/d1606e78c0f8e9cf00a42043d5c3cf97?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"user1751825","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14484/user1751825"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1615795671,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":87099,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"A very important piece of information is missing from your question, that is, why is the child so distressed by the presence of this other parent? The child&#39;s &quot;immense&quot; distress should not be ignored. Since this question also mentions separation, it seems to me that there may be something far more sinister involved here. It&#39;s interesting you use the word &quot;offending&quot; parent. It makes me wonder what &quot;offence&quot; has this parent actually committed?"},{"owner":{"account_id":1946899,"reputation":3943,"user_id":14484,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/d1606e78c0f8e9cf00a42043d5c3cf97?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"user1751825","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14484/user1751825"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1615796165,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":87101,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"If the distress is related to possible abuse, then it can certainly do serious long term harm, whether the child remembers it or not."},{"owner":{"account_id":4594640,"reputation":121,"user_id":40782,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b4e30985026dd7454af031ec615ecd02?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Allure","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40782/allure"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1946899,"reputation":3943,"user_id":14484,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/d1606e78c0f8e9cf00a42043d5c3cf97?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"user1751825","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14484/user1751825"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1615800998,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":87102,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@user1751825 I don&#39;t know, if the offending parent is doing something that causes the distress, they are not telling me what it is. Also, I&#39;m using &quot;offending parent&quot; to mean the parent that is apparently causing the distress; it does not mean that parent is actually committing an offence of some sort."},{"owner":{"account_id":4594640,"reputation":121,"user_id":40782,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b4e30985026dd7454af031ec615ecd02?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Allure","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40782/allure"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1615801016,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":87103,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Is there any evidence abuse before age 2 has long-term effects? If so that would be an answer to this question."},{"owner":{"account_id":10155,"reputation":6280,"user_id":388,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/279c4270deeebe469b7c3770e7accfae?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Kevin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/388/kevin"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1616015280,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":87183,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Voting to close as this is too vague. &quot;Distressed&quot; is to vague a term and there&#39;s no explanation of what is (or might be) causing the distress. A baby crying when a certain person is around could have any number of causes, ranging from the baby not liking the smell of their cologne/perfume to the baby being legitimately fearful due to abuse, or anywhere in between."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1616602789,"post_id":41461,"comment_id":87224,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I don&#39;t blame you for not believing the response you were given. It&#39;s simply wrong. @Timur Shtatland is correct. Simply put, the uterus can expand more (so is larger) at the top, where there are no bones, than the bottom, which is surrounded by ligaments and the bony pelvis. As the bottom half of a baby (buttocks and legs) is larger than the shoulders and arms, and the head smaller yet than those, the head-first presentation is pysiological, and most common."},{"owner":{"account_id":20553,"reputation":10800,"user_id":7798,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/431cd43aaf713caa80316e852a87a840?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Paul Johnson","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7798/paul-johnson"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1617889568,"post_id":41500,"comment_id":88322,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Does she like banana flavoured food in general?"},{"owner":{"account_id":190624,"reputation":2544,"user_id":31496,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0eb07ddb42ad7121a9b57508dd0b89ce?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Bart van Ingen Schenau","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31496/bart-van-ingen-schenau"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1617965812,"post_id":41500,"comment_id":88325,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Does your daughter try to chew the banana, or does her gag-reflex kick in when she tries to swallow the piece whole?"},{"owner":{"account_id":9220582,"reputation":446,"user_id":28934,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c41c1847ed8f71e1041c6d3158f94b57?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"paulj","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/28934/paulj"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":190624,"reputation":2544,"user_id":31496,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0eb07ddb42ad7121a9b57508dd0b89ce?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Bart van Ingen Schenau","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31496/bart-van-ingen-schenau"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1617990174,"post_id":41500,"comment_id":88326,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@BartvanIngenSchenau I believe when she tries to swallow any food, she gags it up. Might not be chewing it?"},{"owner":{"account_id":6397988,"reputation":2514,"user_id":36241,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/96f43840d82f2a5c6d5270549422ac6f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Solar Mike","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36241/solar-mike"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1618602534,"post_id":41500,"comment_id":88335,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Our kids learnt to “chew” by chewing or biting stuff like carrots as they were teething - better than biting bits off toys..."},{"owner":{"account_id":264052,"reputation":13329,"user_id":4087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/X5OLy.png?s=256","display_name":"Becuzz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4087/becuzz"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1618927258,"post_id":41524,"comment_id":88346,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Not to be patronizing, but if the doctor says everything is alright, why are you worried?  Do you not trust this doctor?"},{"owner":{"account_id":504225,"reputation":446,"user_id":40949,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6ca2769dc497a8c037d3c9b434d0c86e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Sean Duggan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40949/sean-duggan"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":264052,"reputation":13329,"user_id":4087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/X5OLy.png?s=256","display_name":"Becuzz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4087/becuzz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1618932035,"post_id":41524,"comment_id":88347,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":":) No, we get that it&#39;s medically OK for him to sleep so much. But we&#39;re also being told to adhere to a regular feeding schedule, which is difficult with him falling asleep during the feeding."},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1618951338,"post_id":41524,"comment_id":88348,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"How old is your baby? And just for clarification: Did your doctor specifically recommend the feeding frequency <i>also through the night</i>? Often enough they <i>mean</i> “through the day” but sort of take it as a given, especially if everything else is “fine” overall."},{"owner":{"account_id":1312213,"reputation":4224,"user_id":9368,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2442ea911c89187262d6575d739a1ca7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"swbarnes2","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9368/swbarnes2"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1619034305,"post_id":41524,"comment_id":88349,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Of course talk to your doctor, but if he&#39;s maintaining his percentile in weight, he&#39;s probably okay.  If he&#39;s dropping percentiles, that&#39;s when doctors worry"},{"owner":{"account_id":190624,"reputation":2544,"user_id":31496,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0eb07ddb42ad7121a9b57508dd0b89ce?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Bart van Ingen Schenau","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31496/bart-van-ingen-schenau"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1619344077,"post_id":41530,"comment_id":88357,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"What does it mean that the season is 6 to 7 months? Does it mean you will be away from home (and your family) for that entire time, or will you be working on and off during that period, being home a number of days every few weeks? Also, do you have colleagues in a similar situation (small children at home) that you can talk to and ask about their experence?"},{"owner":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1619393888,"post_id":41530,"comment_id":88358,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Who will be looking after the kid(s) when you&#39;re away for half of the year? The father? Your parents?"},{"owner":{"account_id":21396500,"reputation":11,"user_id":40998,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://graph.facebook.com/2996224307279191/picture?type=large","display_name":"Cherylin Anne Botha","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40998/cherylin-anne-botha"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1619431399,"post_id":41530,"comment_id":88365,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I would have to be away for the entire time as it is in another country. My parents would be looking after my child and I will also be able to afford a nanny to assist. The father is also working away from home however he is away for almost a year at a time."},{"owner":{"account_id":2443084,"reputation":492,"user_id":16570,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/bcc10f0e4b08d0d5927f07e62456768a?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"DRF","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16570/drf"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1619616662,"post_id":41530,"comment_id":88411,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"While Hilmar&#39;s answer is a very harsh take, I&#39;m afraid it&#39;s very realistic. Especially for a kid this young the amount of time away will be about half the time they&#39;ve been alive. When you first come back you will quite likely be pretty much a stranger to them. More an aunt than a parent. Is it at least realistic for you to at least be present through videoconferencing on a daily basis? It won&#39;t work for a small kid, but might help  a little once they are a bit older. I&#39;d manage expectations though."},{"owner":{"account_id":21396500,"reputation":11,"user_id":40998,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://graph.facebook.com/2996224307279191/picture?type=large","display_name":"Cherylin Anne Botha","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40998/cherylin-anne-botha"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1619623916,"post_id":41530,"comment_id":88412,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Video calls will be a daily priority, it absolutely breaks my heart being thrown into this position where I need to be the sole provider for my children and give them the life they are deserving of. My main goal is to get all 3 of us out of South Africa. This is also a temporary measure as I plan on working this way for 3 to 4 years at most"},{"owner":{"account_id":325145,"reputation":8805,"user_id":27868,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/rX1ss.png?s=256","display_name":"Pascal remembers Monica","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27868/pascal-remembers-monica"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1619629911,"post_id":41530,"comment_id":88420,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Regarding video-conferences with toddlers (age 1 up): <a href=\"https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2016/07/160715115023.htm\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">New study of toddlers sheds light on value of Facetime video chat as meaningful interaction</a> (I found this quite surprising, didn&#39;t think video chat would mean much to toddlers...)"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1620077544,"post_id":41575,"comment_id":88465,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"There&#39;s no valve in the esophagus; that would literally be deadly. (Valves are one-way only. Imagine never being able to burp or vomit when necessary.) There is a sphincter at the gastroesophageal junction (GEJ) through which air can escape. The rest, you&#39;ve got pretty well. You change the baby&#39;s position to try to get the bubble to the GEJ of the stomach as it can sometimes get trapped in the fundus, a dome-like structure."},{"owner":{"account_id":482697,"reputation":4291,"user_id":13387,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/efdd0371ee7b983ab3b858ea92653aeb?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"dsollen","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13387/dsollen"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1620747809,"post_id":41475,"comment_id":88575,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"just a nitpick, but her dad is her real dad.  Adoptive, and step, fathers that have regularly loved and cared for a child are more &#39;father&#39; for a kid that a biological father that doesn&#39;t do anything for the child.  Make sure you don&#39;t use language with your daughter, or husband, that in any way implies that your husband is not her father just because it wasn&#39;t his sperm that made her.  He is the dad in every way that truly matters and you don&#39;t want to confuse her, or potentially offend your husband, by implying anything else."},{"owner":{"account_id":1530716,"reputation":3476,"user_id":36371,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/48248a8939c6db9b3d90f02b72354198?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"stan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36371/stan"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1620748245,"post_id":41573,"comment_id":88576,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"There&#39;s the 5s&#39;s that are a good place to start: <a href=\"https://www.safesleepacademy.org/how-to-soothe-fussy-baby/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">safesleepacademy.org/how-to-soothe-fussy-baby</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1620912676,"post_id":41610,"comment_id":88655,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Comments are not for extended discussion; this conversation has been <a href=\"https://chat.stackexchange.com/rooms/124219/discussion-on-question-by-blarg-grandmother-keeps-calling-my-daughter-good-girl\">moved to chat</a>.Any further discussion should take place there, not in comments."},{"owner":{"account_id":2811843,"reputation":428,"user_id":27701,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/9fe7def9462f2e9e696d8d5c367af484?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"user","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27701/user"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1621356373,"post_id":41610,"comment_id":88686,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It seems to me you and your wife are making a big issue out of nothing"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1621612059,"post_id":41639,"comment_id":88714,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I think he (and you both) sound wonderful. I apologize to my <i>dogs</i> when I bump into them or cause any pain (and do so emphatically, turning to pet them and reassure them it was not intentional), as well as saying &quot;Please&quot; and &quot;Thank you.&quot; I apologize to people in the same manner (well, I don&#39;t pet them...), and don&#39;t consider myself overly apologetic.  But I am most assuredly assertive. Being empathetic and being able to voice your preferences - emphatically if need be -  are (imo) two different abilities."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1621612469,"post_id":41639,"comment_id":88715,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I also think that maybe saying &quot;Thank you for waiting&quot; when it&#39;s actually you who  forgot to do something is <i>kinda</i> but not quite blame-shifting, but can easily move in that direction."},{"owner":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2811843,"reputation":428,"user_id":27701,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/9fe7def9462f2e9e696d8d5c367af484?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"user","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27701/user"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1621924784,"post_id":41610,"comment_id":88731,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@user I recommend heading to <a href=\"https://chat.stackexchange.com/rooms/124219/discussion-on-question-by-blarg-grandmother-keeps-calling-my-daughter-good-girl\">the linked chat</a> for this kind of comment, where that concern has been discussed <i>in almost unplumbable depth</i>.  Nobody should want that tedious and tendentious conversation to end up back on this page."},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user9761"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1622111231,"post_id":41639,"comment_id":88755,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"aw thank you @anongoodnurse I really appreciate that. Thank you, I suppose I could explore how to teach him assertiveness, which will ultimately come from myself... thank you!"},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user36162"},"edited":false,"score":8,"creation_date":1622672566,"post_id":41680,"comment_id":88787,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I think this is more of an interpersonal question than a parental one, and as such unfortunately not a good fit for this site, but for what it&#39;s worth, no that&#39;s not normal. It&#39;s perfectly reasonable not to want to involve your children before you know you&#39;re ready to commit, but getting another child is a bigger commitment still, so that reasoning is wholly inapplicable here."},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1622699219,"post_id":41680,"comment_id":88788,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@dxh agreed - I sense red flags."},{"owner":{"account_id":10500038,"reputation":286,"user_id":32212,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh5.googleusercontent.com/-NmxCFM2PnFY/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAADw/3AKQg-1-89k/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Imus","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32212/imus"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1622714112,"post_id":41680,"comment_id":88789,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"As it is now this question isn&#39;t really a good fit for IPS either. If it&#39;s changed to &quot;how to approach talking about this with my boyfriend&quot; including earlier attempts that failed could be a fit there, but I don&#39;t think this in itself is the question here."},{"owner":{"account_id":680,"reputation":1503,"user_id":9114,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/747ffa5da3538e66840ebc0548b8fd58?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Greg Hewgill","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9114/greg-hewgill"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623095642,"post_id":41691,"comment_id":88816,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Does your daughter have exposure to other extended family members (your wife&#39;s family, presumably) who do not speak English with her?"},{"owner":{"account_id":6791317,"reputation":1624,"user_id":27624,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/eAfikIvI.png?s=256","display_name":"Luke Sawczak","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27624/luke-sawczak"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623208894,"post_id":41691,"comment_id":88833,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"So your wife wants to relegate Spanish to a school subject. Well, every subject at school must be supported at home. You&#39;ll have to help your daughter with math and geography, and you&#39;ll have to help her with Spanish. That means speaking and helping her practice, too. Just as my parents helped me with French in our English-speaking household (which, by the way, didn&#39;t compromise my English one bit). There is good research on the importance of parents showing their children how valuable and interesting their school learning is, with measurable results in the student&#39;s interest and performance."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623248691,"post_id":41695,"comment_id":88840,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Who else lives in the household? Is the cousin&#39;s mother at home? What  (if anything) does she do when this happens? Have you discussed this behavior with her?"},{"owner":{"account_id":1478441,"reputation":143,"user_id":40399,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/mfJ7p.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Jacques Gaudin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40399/jacques-gaudin"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1623754051,"post_id":41707,"comment_id":88881,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"My 7 month daughter needs a 10 mins cuddle after her last meal to burp and be ready to sleep. I do it in a dark room with gentle music and when she&#39;s about to fall asleep, I lay her down into her crib. Then she falls asleep on her own. If I lay her down to early, she has gas and vomits. Hope it&#39;s helpful."},{"owner":{"account_id":85750,"reputation":1992,"user_id":16114,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/8ca29123b5669b26f9b6ca2c62d9d215?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Wayne Conrad","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16114/wayne-conrad"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623807172,"post_id":41575,"comment_id":88883,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anongoodnurse Is this a difference between medical phraseology and vernacular?  In the vernacular, &quot;valve&quot; does not imply one-way."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":85750,"reputation":1992,"user_id":16114,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/8ca29123b5669b26f9b6ca2c62d9d215?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Wayne Conrad","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16114/wayne-conrad"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1623809318,"post_id":41575,"comment_id":88884,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@WayneConrad - From the dictionary: &quot;a device for controlling the passage of fluid or air through a pipe, duct, etc., especially an automatic device allowing movement in <i>one direction only</i>.&quot; (emphasis mine) I had to look it up, because to me, valves are one way only on/in things with which I&#39;m familiar."},{"owner":{"account_id":6397988,"reputation":2514,"user_id":36241,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/96f43840d82f2a5c6d5270549422ac6f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Solar Mike","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36241/solar-mike"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1625323720,"post_id":41691,"comment_id":88937,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"See this and we did thr opposite : <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/a/39653/36241\">parenting.stackexchange.com/a/39653/36241</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1627564066,"post_id":41819,"comment_id":89089,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"You already know yelling doesn&#39;t help. (It rarely does.) I know this sounds backwards, but have you tried restraining his feet when he kicks? Literal restraints, not your hands holding them still. Two years is old enough to put an act and a consequence together."},{"owner":{"account_id":15359808,"reputation":161,"user_id":40103,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/VPdoF.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Tyler Church","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40103/tyler-church"},"edited":false,"score":6,"creation_date":1628609500,"post_id":41852,"comment_id":89163,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Not a full answer but more of a frame challenge - I have never found it helpful to think of newborns as &quot;stubborn&quot;. They just spent 9 months being constantly snuggled and provided for by their mom, and now they&#39;re awash in a world of new sensations and problems they&#39;ve never had before. I would be freaking out too! As an engineer, you may appreciate the idea that some problems are easier to solve if you reframe them. Look for a new way of viewing this situation."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1628639048,"post_id":41852,"comment_id":89166,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I know you&#39;re using humor to describe your circumstances, but some of it may be misinterpreted. <b>Many weeks</b> of near-sleepless nights are as common as babies themselves.  If you search this site for &quot;newborn&quot; and &quot;sleep&quot;, you&#39;ll see that 1) the problem is nearly universal/guaranteed and 2) no single answer fits every child. Right now, even though you&#39;re exhausted,  <i>baby&#39;s needs come first</i>. Read about swaddling, wearing baby, skin-to-skin contact, and the many things that help soothe this miraculous little creature <i>you</i> made."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1628639358,"post_id":41852,"comment_id":89167,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Just a small fyi to let you know I&#39;ve been there: I remember the exuberant entry I made in my firstborn&#39;s &quot;first year&quot; book when they slept for more than 2 hours straight... at 5 weeks! The first time they slept through the night didn&#39;t make it into that book or the next."},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1628682097,"post_id":41852,"comment_id":89170,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"At three weeks (and for quite a while longer), you are not dealing with a “stubborn” baby, but simply a newborn with a lot of different needs and very limited communication skills."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1629309219,"post_id":41867,"comment_id":89197,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Parents have swung their children by the arms for generations without ill effects. It&#39;s an overabundance of caution to warn against this out of concern for nursemaid&#39;s elbow (NE). Hanging from monkey bars is the same. <b>N.B.</b> In both instances, <b>both arms</b> bear the body&#39;s weight. NEs (which are as common as dirt) are caused by sudden jerking of <i>one arm</i>, as in preventing a child from falling/running into danger, lifting a child by one arm, yanking on a kid&#39;s arm out of frustration, etc. Once a child has had it, though, they are quite prone to recurrence, so those kids are restricted."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1629309352,"post_id":41867,"comment_id":89198,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"See <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/a/38922/9327\">parenting.stackexchange.com/a/38922/9327</a>."},{"owner":{"account_id":3203153,"reputation":119,"user_id":39692,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/31xfi.jpg?s=256","display_name":"sam_smith","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39692/sam-smith"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1629601299,"post_id":41867,"comment_id":89224,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@anongoodnurse this comment would be a good answer - it answers the question well focusing on the normal worry (NE) and the risk for a child changing by both arms (low) while hanging. It also links well to the cause of NE being a yanking motion rather than a upward force that hanging would cause"},{"owner":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629690594,"post_id":41883,"comment_id":89225,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Have you talked to your paediatrician about it?"},{"owner":{"account_id":920526,"reputation":1885,"user_id":32290,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/vPzRc.png?s=256","display_name":"Flater","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32290/flater"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629704099,"post_id":41883,"comment_id":89228,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@nick012000: We went to the pediatrician the day before it started happening because he <i>wasn&#39;t</i> sleeping very well. It took us a while to figure out the right balance of feeding/meds to solve cramps/diarrhaea issues. An appt takes a few days and I need to book leave from work, and it seems a bit futile to now go back and basically go &quot;now he sleeps well. What now?&quot; That&#39;s not to say that I wouldn&#39;t take him if necessary, but I&#39;m rather trying to figure out whether there is any indication to do so, as sleeping <i>more</i> doesn&#39;t strike me as cause for concern. Hence the question to make sure."},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1629725951,"post_id":41883,"comment_id":89230,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Let sleeping dogs lie. And sleeping babies even more so."},{"owner":{"account_id":20553,"reputation":10800,"user_id":7798,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/431cd43aaf713caa80316e852a87a840?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Paul Johnson","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7798/paul-johnson"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1629727677,"post_id":41883,"comment_id":89231,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Does he seem alert when he&#39;s awake during the day? If so then I wouldn&#39;t worry."},{"owner":{"account_id":920526,"reputation":1885,"user_id":32290,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/vPzRc.png?s=256","display_name":"Flater","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32290/flater"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":20553,"reputation":10800,"user_id":7798,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/431cd43aaf713caa80316e852a87a840?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Paul Johnson","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7798/paul-johnson"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629731456,"post_id":41883,"comment_id":89232,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@PaulJohnson: He seems a bit behind on the curve at starting to vocalize and interact with us (my wife tells me that should be happening by week 6), but he is perfectly awake during the day."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1629773267,"post_id":41883,"comment_id":89233,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"If your baby is following the growth curves, let him sleep. This is probably your first, no? Babies don&#39;t follow rules. Don&#39;t worry if he&#39;s s bit behind in babbling, tracking, etc. This is what your doctor is for, and he should have an 8 week visit coming up soon. When he gives you his first belly-laugh, you&#39;ll forget everything you worried about. Enjoy!"},{"owner":{"account_id":190624,"reputation":2544,"user_id":31496,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0eb07ddb42ad7121a9b57508dd0b89ce?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Bart van Ingen Schenau","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31496/bart-van-ingen-schenau"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1629971082,"post_id":41883,"comment_id":89284,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"To give you another data point, our child was sleeping through the entire night after only 1 week and none of the medical professionals involved were worried about that in any way."},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1630101811,"post_id":41883,"comment_id":89296,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Humorous hint: If you hit the jackpot in the baby-sleeps-through-the-night lottery, please refrain from elaborating on this when talking to parents whose (older) babies are on the other side of the normal distribution curve. Their sleep-deprived brains will have difficulties handling that information in a positive and polite manner. Been there… not proud of my reactions."},{"owner":{"account_id":5962695,"reputation":183,"user_id":27841,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/cDBWJ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"henning no longer feeds AI","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27841/henning-no-longer-feeds-ai"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1630332446,"post_id":41901,"comment_id":89298,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"This behavior is called &quot;clustering&quot; or &quot;<a href=\"https://www.pregnancybirthbaby.org.au/cluster-feeding\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">cluster feeding</a>&quot;. It&#39;s stressful for the mother but not bad for the baby."},{"owner":{"account_id":12596817,"reputation":476,"user_id":32568,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/sUEEh.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Pam","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32568/pam"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":5962695,"reputation":183,"user_id":27841,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/cDBWJ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"henning no longer feeds AI","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27841/henning-no-longer-feeds-ai"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1630347379,"post_id":41901,"comment_id":89300,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@henning, with respect, I’d say it’s not quite clear from the question if this is cluster feeding or not. I’ve always taken &quot;cluster feeding&quot; to be a short term thing (a few hours of on/off in an evening, maybe every evening for a while, say). Regardless, my conclusion is the same: not bad for the baby. Inconvenient for the mother, but it’ll pass."},{"owner":{"account_id":5962695,"reputation":183,"user_id":27841,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/cDBWJ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"henning no longer feeds AI","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27841/henning-no-longer-feeds-ai"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":12596817,"reputation":476,"user_id":32568,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/sUEEh.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Pam","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32568/pam"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1630348659,"post_id":41901,"comment_id":89301,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Pam I see. Perhaps it&#39;s shades of grey."},{"owner":{"account_id":22602663,"reputation":161,"user_id":41698,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJxKQj1__hYvurlACGyIpv5_oO5EnJl0XOuJt3wn=k-s256","display_name":"Courtney Lozada","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41698/courtney-lozada"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1630350765,"post_id":41901,"comment_id":89302,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Hello sorry I didn’t clarify. My son feeds every hour every single day he’ll feed for 5 minutes unlatch then the next hour he’ll feed again. The only time he doesn’t feed every hour is in the middle of the night he feeds maybe every 4 hours"},{"owner":{"account_id":12596817,"reputation":476,"user_id":32568,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/sUEEh.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Pam","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32568/pam"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1630352202,"post_id":41901,"comment_id":89303,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@CourtneyLozada, then I’d +2 the answer below if I could! Your baby has found a custom routine that currently (mostly!) works for both of you. Don’t worry about the clock (unless there are other signs something is wrong)."},{"owner":{"account_id":4594640,"reputation":121,"user_id":40782,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b4e30985026dd7454af031ec615ecd02?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Allure","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40782/allure"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":10155,"reputation":6280,"user_id":388,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/279c4270deeebe469b7c3770e7accfae?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Kevin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/388/kevin"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1630933186,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":89336,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Kevin it appears probable (see update) that the distress has no easily-described cause, and in fact has no apparent cause at all."},{"owner":{"account_id":10155,"reputation":6280,"user_id":388,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/279c4270deeebe469b7c3770e7accfae?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Kevin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/388/kevin"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1631021434,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":89338,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Allure In any event, if it takes multiple child psychologists to diagnose the problem, I think it is beyond the stage of being answerable by someone on this site"},{"owner":{"account_id":4594640,"reputation":121,"user_id":40782,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b4e30985026dd7454af031ec615ecd02?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Allure","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40782/allure"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":10155,"reputation":6280,"user_id":388,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/279c4270deeebe469b7c3770e7accfae?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Kevin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/388/kevin"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1631045359,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":89339,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Kevin this question isn&#39;t about what the problem is, though. I only updated the question with that explanation because certain comments (like yours) asked for it."},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1631805716,"post_id":41928,"comment_id":89348,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"What have you done so far to increase his strength and encourage moving? I am thinking about holding him just by his sides, placing him  under a plaything with dangling toys, giving him tummy time… in short, how much of his time does he spend on his back or in a stroller, car seat, carrier, …"},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1631807466,"post_id":41928,"comment_id":89349,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"And as you talked to your pediatrician: Did he exclude any issues that could cause delays in development, in other words, is your baby healthy and just untrained (or “lazy” as you put it)?"},{"owner":{"account_id":19170026,"reputation":81,"user_id":39221,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/91aa1adb0e8f50f17add990d43cd8ad6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"buck","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39221/buck"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1631813145,"post_id":41928,"comment_id":89350,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Hello，Stephie: the pediatrician did not mention any health issues of my baby. He looks healthy and smart. Only the gross motor skills are lagging, other skills such as fine motor and speech are good."},{"owner":{"account_id":19170026,"reputation":81,"user_id":39221,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/91aa1adb0e8f50f17add990d43cd8ad6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"buck","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39221/buck"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1631813601,"post_id":41928,"comment_id":89351,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"He spends most of his time on his back or sit. If I put him on tammy, he will roll over. I tried to attract his attention with different objects,  build him a slope and encourage him to crawl down, dangling him up with a towel and put him into a crawling position, personally demostrate to him, and also bought him a crawling toy baby to teach him. He doesn&#39;t seem to understand what I want him to do."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1631936505,"post_id":41928,"comment_id":89357,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Crawling down is not feasible, as their legs are much stronger than their arms, and their weight would be behind them. They likely would not even try it. It&#39;s not laziness, it&#39;s physics., once he can crawl. Crawling up would be more likely to succeed. Also, you say he has good fine motor skills. If he doesn&#39;t like toys, what does he do besides feed himself? Then tempt him with food!"},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":5,"creation_date":1632578823,"post_id":41948,"comment_id":89389,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The totally non-scientific answer from a mother of two: Heck, yes, it exists. In my experience it’s less about being “too late” on a regular day, but happens when there’s A lot of unusual activity going on - going on a visit, for example - and the mind struggles to process all the impressions. And <i>that</i> is a phenomenon I as an adult have also experienced: The body is tired, the mind is racing. (Which would mean the age bracket is 0-100, with decreasing severity.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":2067036,"reputation":8662,"user_id":3527,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/27caf1b4834e7f79a4bc8af5890424ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Hilmar","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/3527/hilmar"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1632660198,"post_id":41948,"comment_id":89390,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Same here. Anecdotally it clearly exisits"},{"owner":{"account_id":431108,"reputation":101,"user_id":27211,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7d088f6dc438b42d2aef9770c998f4f4?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Witness Protection ID 44583292","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27211/witness-protection-id-44583292"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1632704850,"post_id":41949,"comment_id":89396,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Here on the central California coast, our oak trees frequently have poison oak bushes and runners growing alongside or nearby, and the leaves are similar and easily confused, although looking closely reveals the oak leaves have pointed lobes while poison oak leaves have rounded lobes.  Other than for a few immune people, poison oak leaves are definitely not safe to touch."},{"owner":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1632739895,"post_id":41949,"comment_id":89400,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<a href=\"https://cooking.stackexchange.com/questions/110725/collecting-acorns-to-eat-how-when\">Here&#39;s</a> a bit more on eating acorns, especially as you&#39;re in the UK - we had loads last year and I made cookies (good) and ersatz coffee (not so good).  The same tannins that irritate the digestion would also cause any sensible baby to spit out the leaf pretty quickly"},{"owner":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":431108,"reputation":101,"user_id":27211,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7d088f6dc438b42d2aef9770c998f4f4?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Witness Protection ID 44583292","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27211/witness-protection-id-44583292"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1632739981,"post_id":41949,"comment_id":89402,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@WitnessProtectionID44583292 since your comment the question has a UK tag, so poison oak isn&#39;t an issue - it doesn&#39;t exist here"},{"owner":{"account_id":15983773,"reputation":195,"user_id":41799,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/-vy9bO7mC9NU/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAACI/s12PH9aZo9E/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Ed HP","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41799/ed-hp"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1632745839,"post_id":41949,"comment_id":89405,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Chris H I added the UK tag after seeing WitnessProtectionID44583292 &#39;s comment :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1632745877,"post_id":41949,"comment_id":89406,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@EdHP I suspected as much"},{"owner":{"account_id":1086299,"reputation":257,"user_id":16319,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/B5cTn.png?s=256","display_name":"terdon","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16319/terdon"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1632756961,"post_id":41949,"comment_id":89415,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"May I ask why you think oak would be any more dangerous than fig or almond tree or any other type of leaf? Is there some special reason you think oak, specifically would be dangerous? Is it because ingesting large quantities can be dangerous or is there something else? I grew up in Greece where we don&#39;t really have much dangerous flora, and I&#39;ve heard of &quot;poison oak&quot; but I associate it with the Americas."},{"owner":{"account_id":15983773,"reputation":195,"user_id":41799,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/-vy9bO7mC9NU/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAACI/s12PH9aZo9E/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Ed HP","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41799/ed-hp"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1086299,"reputation":257,"user_id":16319,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/B5cTn.png?s=256","display_name":"terdon","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16319/terdon"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1632761154,"post_id":41949,"comment_id":89417,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@terdon It was only after handing her that leaf that I got thinking about it.  I was curious around oak leaves in particular, but it could have been any other leaf if I&#39;d picked that up first!"},{"owner":{"account_id":474597,"reputation":229,"user_id":6593,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/93234f6c046fc1554f9fb2c471fb09ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Carl Witthoft","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6593/carl-witthoft"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1633007013,"post_id":41967,"comment_id":89444,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Related:  be careful bringing home your <i>first</i> baby if you have cats or dogs in your house.  While most animals will accept it, some are similarly jealous of the baby taking their(the animal&#39;s) spot as most favored child."},{"owner":{"account_id":20013021,"reputation":381,"user_id":41810,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e9f90a3d760a38895dceaeec309ab06e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"anna brooks","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41810/anna-brooks"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":474597,"reputation":229,"user_id":6593,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/93234f6c046fc1554f9fb2c471fb09ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Carl Witthoft","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6593/carl-witthoft"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633007976,"post_id":41967,"comment_id":89449,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The book I bought is Best Ever Big Sister, for reference!"},{"owner":{"account_id":20013021,"reputation":381,"user_id":41810,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e9f90a3d760a38895dceaeec309ab06e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"anna brooks","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41810/anna-brooks"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":474597,"reputation":229,"user_id":6593,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/93234f6c046fc1554f9fb2c471fb09ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Carl Witthoft","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6593/carl-witthoft"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633008008,"post_id":41967,"comment_id":89450,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@CarlWitthoft I do have a senior dog and two American Staffordshire Mixes, I will keep this in mind"},{"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":44,"creation_date":1633008184,"post_id":41967,"comment_id":89451,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I find it somewhat shocking that the parents weren&#39;t willing to tell their children themselves - even at a young age like this, hearing the news from a stranger instead of their own parent could be a big reason why it was taken so poorly."},{"owner":{"account_id":20013021,"reputation":381,"user_id":41810,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e9f90a3d760a38895dceaeec309ab06e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"anna brooks","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41810/anna-brooks"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1633013099,"post_id":41967,"comment_id":89452,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Zibbobz agreed! thats the reason that she isn&#39;t talking to her mother, from what I can gather from her sister"},{"owner":{"account_id":190624,"reputation":2544,"user_id":31496,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0eb07ddb42ad7121a9b57508dd0b89ce?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Bart van Ingen Schenau","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31496/bart-van-ingen-schenau"},"edited":false,"score":30,"creation_date":1633353763,"post_id":41984,"comment_id":89480,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"What forms/sources of radiation are you worried about? What kind of thermal effects are you worried about?"},{"owner":{"account_id":11357901,"reputation":316,"user_id":41684,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a233664230524227966976d885423b04?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"QuestionablePresence","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41684/questionablepresence"},"edited":false,"score":5,"creation_date":1633429103,"post_id":41984,"comment_id":89492,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Not worried about constant light and noise but about EM radiation?"},{"owner":{"account_id":474597,"reputation":229,"user_id":6593,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/93234f6c046fc1554f9fb2c471fb09ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Carl Witthoft","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6593/carl-witthoft"},"edited":false,"score":18,"creation_date":1633443945,"post_id":41984,"comment_id":89496,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It is a shame that the educational process for software experts doesnt include basic electronics, which would eliminate confusion at this level."},{"owner":{"account_id":8917701,"reputation":101,"user_id":32570,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7096c9a8c4f9f550983e1b472ee95538?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Pablo H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32570/pablo-h"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1633452296,"post_id":41984,"comment_id":89505,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Apart from the noise from (rotating) hard disks and fans, some electronic devices make high-pitch low-volume noise. You probably don&#39;t notice that noise anymore. A little kid does notice such noises, sometimes (anecdotal evidence: lightbulb). Noise might make babies and kids nervous/restless (without apparent reason unless you notice the noise)."},{"owner":{"account_id":22957548,"reputation":1,"user_id":41846,"user_type":"unregistered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f1aa0990a1f6e4b19467e7d1c16d6ceb?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Thomas","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41846/thomas"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633511983,"post_id":41984,"comment_id":89529,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Adding to other answers: What you should not use in same room as baby are printers. Inkjets just &#39;smell&#39;, but laser printers create ozone, which is not healthy. Take the printing outside the room."},{"owner":{"account_id":1530716,"reputation":3476,"user_id":36371,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/48248a8939c6db9b3d90f02b72354198?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"stan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36371/stan"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1633517809,"post_id":41948,"comment_id":89527,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Even adults get overtired. I find there&#39;s a period about around 9:30-10ish (my usual bedtime) when I get absolutely exhaused for ~30 min or so. If I push through till 10:30, I will be fine to stay up until midnight (and in fact, going to bed during that time is difficult). I think it has something to do with melatonin levels and when it is released in the body."},{"owner":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1633519777,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":89530,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"In light of your updates, we&#39;ll still need to keep this closed. They went to a professional and they couldn&#39;t help - we on an internet Q&amp;A site will not have better input than that."},{"owner":{"account_id":4594640,"reputation":121,"user_id":40782,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b4e30985026dd7454af031ec615ecd02?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Allure","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40782/allure"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633520663,"post_id":41374,"comment_id":89534,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@RoryAlsop the question isn&#39;t asking for help with their situation, though (see the three questions that are actually asked). I only added those details because some people wanted to know why the baby is crying."},{"owner":{"account_id":5570295,"reputation":346,"user_id":28666,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cacf59af8a8aeffa8229f913e5d7015b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Caterpillaraoz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/28666/caterpillaraoz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1633542234,"post_id":41984,"comment_id":89541,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;d be more concerned about the vaporization of all the anti-flame stuff on the boards"},{"owner":{"account_id":3654094,"reputation":101,"user_id":29230,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/f5YBIEo6.png?s=256","display_name":"maxathousand","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/29230/maxathousand"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":474597,"reputation":229,"user_id":6593,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/93234f6c046fc1554f9fb2c471fb09ec?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Carl Witthoft","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6593/carl-witthoft"},"edited":false,"score":7,"creation_date":1633545030,"post_id":41984,"comment_id":89543,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@CarlWitthoft Even if the software curriculum did include cursory electronics courses, this seems like a perfectly reasonable question for a new parent to ask. Others here probably know more than what is taught in a one-semester Introduction to Electronics course. OP should be encouraged to continue asking questions, not berated for not knowing something you may find obvious."},{"owner":{"account_id":2616278,"reputation":101,"user_id":21091,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/oIQS1.png?s=256","display_name":"Mołot","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/21091/mo%c5%82ot"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1633735135,"post_id":41948,"comment_id":89551,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I experienced this from kindergarten to curent day. For me it exists, my poor mom, my poor wife..."}],"has_more":true,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":274}